({#dUzayati-te#}, but {#dUzayati-te#}
 or {#dozayati-te#} in the sense of ‘making depraved’ or ‘corrupting’)
.²1 To corrupt, spoil, cause to perish, hurt, destroy, defile, taint, contaminate,
 vitiate, pollute (<ab>lit.</ab> and <ab>fig.</ab>); {#na BIto maraRAdasmi kevalaM dUzitaM#}
 {#yaSaH#} <ls>Mk. 10. 27</ls>; {#pUrA dUzayati sTalIm#} <ls>R. 12. 30</ls>, <ls n="R.">8. 68</ls>, <ls n="R.">10. 47</ls>, <ls n="R.">12. 4</ls>;
 <ls>Ms. 5. 1</ls>, <ls n="Ms. 5.">104</ls>, <ls n="Ms.">7. 195</ls>; <ls>Y. 1. 189</ls>; <ls>Amaru. 72</ls>; {#na tvevaM dUzayizyAmi#}
 {#SastragrahamahAvratam#} <ls>Mv. 3. 8</ls> ‘shall not sully, violate or break <ab>&c.</ab>’
.²2 To corrupt the morals, demoralize.
.²3 To violate or dishonour (as a girl or anotherʼs wife); {#yo'kAmAM dUzayetkanyAM#}
 {#sa sadyo vaDamarhati#} <ls>Ms. 8. 364</ls>, <ls n="Ms. 8.">368</ls>.
.²4 To abrogate, rescind, annul.
.²5 To blame, censure, find fault with, speak ill of, accuse; {#dUzitaH#}
 {#sarvalokezu nizAdatvaM gamizyati#} <ls>Rām.</ls>; <ls>Y. 1. 66</ls>.
.²6 To adulterate.
.²7 To falsify.
.²8 To refute, disprove.
